19:T620,## When a class varies along two axes

Some things change in **two dimensions at once**. A `Shape` can be a circle or a square, *and* it can be red, blue, or green. A `Remote` can be basic or advanced, *and* it can drive a TV or a radio.

If you model both axes with subclassing you get the **cartesian product**: `RedCircle`, `BlueCircle`, `RedSquare`, `BlueSquare`, … 3 shapes × 4 colours = **12 classes**, and every new colour multiplies the lot again. This is the **N×M explosion**.

## Separate the two hierarchies

The **Bridge** pattern splits the one tangled hierarchy into two:

- the **abstraction**, `Shape` (what the client talks to), and
- the **implementation**, `Color` (the varying detail it delegates to).

Each becomes its own independent hierarchy, and they're connected by **composition**, the abstraction holds a reference to an implementation. That reference *is* the "bridge".

```
abstract class Shape {
    protected Color color;                 // the bridge
    Shape(Color c){ this.color = c; }
    abstract void draw();
}
class Circle extends Shape { void draw(){ color.fill(); /* circle */ } }
Shape s = new Circle(new Blue());          // combined at runtime
```

## Why it's powerful

Now M shapes **+** N colours = **M + N** classes, not M × N. Combine any shape with any colour **at runtime**, and adding a colour is **one new class**, not N.

> Bridge differs from **Adapter**: Bridge is designed **up front** to let two hierarchies vary independently; Adapter **retrofits** an incompatible interface onto code you already have.c:["$","div",null,{"className":"flex min-h-screen","children":[["$","$L17",null,{"activeModule":"lld-structural","activeLesson":"bridge"}],["$","$L18","bridge",{"lesson":{"id":"bridge","patternId":"lld-structural","title":"Bridge","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"Advanced","statement":"Split one thing that varies in TWO dimensions into two separate hierarchies, joined by a reference, so each can grow independently instead of multiplying.","companies":["Amazon","Microsoft","Adobe"],"theory":"$19","approaches":[{"label":"N×M explosion","code":["class Shape { ... }","class RedCircle  extends Shape { ... }","class BlueCircle extends Shape { ... }","class RedSquare  extends Shape { ... }   // … and on, and on","// 3 shapes × 4 colours = 12 classes 💥"],"steps":[{"view":"class","codeLine":2,"narration":"Subclass every shape × colour combination: RedCircle, BlueCircle, RedSquare… each pairing needs its very own class.","classDiagram":{"classes":[{"id":"shape","name":"Shape","x":1,"y":0.1},{"id":"rc","name":"RedCircle","x":0,"y":1.3,"tint":"red"},{"id":"bc","name":"BlueCircle","x":1,"y":1.3,"tint":"red"},{"id":"rs","name":"RedSquare","x":2,"y":1.3,"tint":"red"}],"relations":[{"id":"e1","from":"rc","to":"shape","kind":"inherit"},{"id":"e2","from":"bc","to":"shape","kind":"inherit"},{"id":"e3","from":"rs","to":"shape","kind":"inherit"}],"shownClassIds":["shape","rc","bc","rs"],"shownRelIds":["e1","e2","e3"],"activeIds":["rc","bc","rs"]}},{"view":"class","codeLine":5,"narration":"Two axes vary at once, so the classes multiply: 3 shapes × 4 colours = 12 classes. Add one colour and it grows by a whole row. The hierarchy explodes multiplicatively.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["shape","rc","bc","rs"],"shownRelIds":["e1","e2","e3"],"activeIds":["rc","bc","rs"]},"vars":[["3 × 4","12 classes 💥"]]}]},{"label":"Bridge the two hierarchies","code":["abstract class Shape {","    protected Color color;                 // the bridge","    Shape(Color c){ this.color = c; }","    abstract void draw();","}","interface Color { void fill(); }","class Circle extends Shape  { void draw(){ color.fill(); } }","class Red implements Color  { void fill(){ ... } }","Shape s = new Circle(new Blue());          // any × any"],"steps":[{"view":"class","codeLine":1,"narration":"Shape is the abstraction the client talks to, and crucially it HOLDS a Color (the hollow diamond). That reference is the bridge between the two hierarchies.","classDiagram":{"classes":[{"id":"shape","name":"Shape","stereotype":"abstract","fields":["- color: Color"],"methods":["+ draw()"],"x":0.3,"y":0.2,"tint":"accent"},{"id":"circle","name":"Circle","x":0,"y":1.5},{"id":"square","name":"Square","x":1,"y":1.5},{"id":"color","name":"Color","stereotype":"interface","methods":["+ fill()"],"x":2.3,"y":0.2,"tint":"accent2"},{"id":"red","name":"Red","x":1.9,"y":1.5},{"id":"blue","name":"Blue","x":2.7,"y":1.5}],"relations":[{"id":"b1","from":"circle","to":"shape","kind":"inherit"},{"id":"b2","from":"square","to":"shape","kind":"inherit"},{"id":"b3","from":"red","to":"color","kind":"implement"},{"id":"b4","from":"blue","to":"color","kind":"implement"},{"id":"b5","from":"shape","to":"color","kind":"aggregate","label":"bridge"}],"shownClassIds":["shape","color"],"shownRelIds":["b5"],"activeIds":["shape"]},"vars":[["bridge","Shape holds a Color"]]},{"view":"class","codeLine":7,"narration":"Circle and Square refine the Shape hierarchy, the “what shape” axis, without knowing a single colour.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches: